WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.160
Holding a tablet that was written
thousands of years ago

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.240
and being able to read what it says

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:08.520
is an amazing feeling.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.080
If you see a cuneiform tablet
for the first time

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.200
you're not likely to identify it
as writing

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.600
and you certainly wouldn't know
which way up it went.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.080
It's a form of time travel -

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.600
it catapults you back in time,
thousands of years

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.280
and puts you directly into
the shoes of somebody

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:25.320
who lived so many years before us.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:33.560
The earliest known form of writing
is called cuneiform.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.640
First used over 5000 years ago

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.560
it's believed to predate
Egyptian hieroglyphs.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:44.600
Cuneiform was used by civilisations
that lived in Mesopatamia.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:48.080
Several societies used cuneiform
as their writing system

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:51.000
including the Sumerians
and the Akkadians.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:56.240
Pressed onto clay, cuneiform tablets
are incredibly durable,

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.160
they're literally fireproof,

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:01.720
but for thousands of years,
no one was able to translate them.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:04.480
After much trial and error,

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.920
cuneiform script was finally
deciphered in the Victorian era.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:10.960
What they revealed was extraordinary.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.600
Once cuneiform was deciphered

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.520
lots of unexpected things
came to light

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:20.320
but probably none
which had greater impact

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.400
than the discovery by George Smith
in 1872

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.080
of the 11th tablet
of the Epic of Gilgamesh

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.640
in which he encountered
for the first time, the flood story.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.480
Finding an ancient tablet with the
story of Noah's Ark

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.800
written hundreds of years
before the Bible

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:40.680
shattered the Victorian's
understanding of the world.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:46.080
When it arrived, it was a huge...
...bang, thing like that.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:47.800
It was a very explosive matter.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:52.280
And the parallel was much more
than a sort of, general similarity

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:54.280
with a boat and water and animals.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.440
It was in the same order

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:00.440
and there were many close points that
compellingly showed

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:04.000
that this same story had been current
in Mesopotamia

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:07.640
a millennium before the earliest date

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:11.160
when the Hebrew text is likely to
have come into existence.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:15.000
It wasn't easy
being a woman in Mesopotamia

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:18.200
but women in wealthy families
were treated fairly well.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:20.560
The first known author
in all of recorded history

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:21.960
is actually a woman.

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:24.760
The Akkadian priestess, Enheduanna.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:26.920
The case of Enheduanna shows us

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.080
that women could reach extremely high
and important positions

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:31.680
in Mesopotamian religion.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:36.720
We learn a lot about society,
about beliefs

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:39.400
relations between husband and wife

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.120
business transactions going wrong.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:46.560
We know from cuneiform tablets
that women had agency.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:49.560
We have contracts where they are
allowed to buy houses

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:51.680
and they retain control
of their dowry.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:54.600
They can run and manage businesses
in their own right

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:56.560
as long as alongside their husbands.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:00.480
If you've ever wondered
why there's 60 seconds in a minute

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:03.320
or 360 degrees in a circle

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.600
it's because
the Sumerians and Akkadians

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:08.160
used a numbering system
that was sexagesimal.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:12.000
Which means that they counted
on a base of 60

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.480
and divisions of 60
and multiplications by 60

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:17.880
where we tend to use
the decimal system.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.320
Our own time measurement into 60
seconds in a minute

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:22.880
and 60 minutes in an hour

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:28.920
is a direct inheritance from
Mesopotamian scholarly tradition.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.480
It's amazing how many concepts
we take for granted

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:33.040
in our modern society

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:36.280
can actually be found for the first
time in ancient Mesopotamia.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.960
The whole concept
of mathematical models

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:41.600
the very idea that you can use data

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:44.240
to predict things happening
in the future

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:47.000
and that's foundational
to all modern science.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:51.280
The Mesopotamians
were keen letter writers

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.280
sending sealed messages
with traders and travellers.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:56.600
Reading these letters today,

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:59.920
you realise that in many ways,
not much has changed.

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:06.080
We can see that there were specific
formulae in their correspondence.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:08.800
As we start an email today by
"I hope all is well"

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:11.360
they also started
with specific formulae.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:14.640
But when they were angry

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:17.080
they forgot about
this formulaic convention

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:21.360
and they just started the letter
very matter-of-factly.

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:23.320
As well as writing about
stock levels,

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:25.640
taxes and receipts on their tablets

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:28.200
cuneiform writers loved to gossip.

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:29.960
We do have letters
from these women

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:33.720
complaining that the men
are not sending enough money home.

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:37.120
We have a certain sense
of keeping up with the Joneses

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.480
saying "next door has built
an extension to their house,

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:42.360
when are we going to have the money
to build an extension to our house?"

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:44.720
So these kind of things
really do come through

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:46.880
and we see
these little human concerns

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:49.040
these little human squabbles

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:52.040
desires, jealousies and so on.

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:54.800
By studying the past

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:58.240
we learn so much about ourselves
and the world that we live in.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:01.160
But the secrets revealed
in cuneiform tablets

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:04.920
are only known to us today
because of clay's durability.

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:08.920
The way that we record things
is constantly evolving.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:12.680
Technological progress means
things become obsolete very quickly.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:16.520
The messages we send every day
are stored in the cloud.

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:20.120
How likely is it that anyone will be
able to read that in 20 years

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:22.160
let alone a few thousand years?

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:24.480
There is a project in Austria

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:29.440
which is inscribing 1000 of
the most important books of our era

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:31.600
onto ceramic tablets.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:34.080
So humanity has really
come full circle

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:37.200
from writing on clay
at the very beginning of history

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:39.160
to writing on clay again

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:42.280
in a different way
to preserve our information now.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:47.200
There are many initiatives trying to
prevent digital data from being lost.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:50.760
Could it be that despite
all the incredible technology

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:52.600
we have at our fingertips

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:55.080
ancient methods
of recording information

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:57.480
are the best way
of preserving our secrets

00:05:57.560 --> 00:05:59.360
for generations to come.

